# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Line endings, made explicit.
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS NOT OPTIONAL HERE
# ---------------------------------
# This repository is developed on Windows (OneDrive checkout) and built on Linux
# (GitHub Actions). Without this file, git applies its OS default in both
# directions: files get CRLF in the working tree on Windows and LF on the
# runner. Git then stores whatever the contributor's core.autocrlf said, so the
# same logical line can be stored two different ways.
#
# The symptom is not subtle. The first person to touch a 400-line file on the
# other platform produces a 400-line diff to change one line. That diff hides the
# real change, defeats `git blame`, and is exactly what makes a reviewer skim a
# commit instead of reading it.
#
# The rule below is the standard, boring, correct answer: normalise to LF IN THE
# REPOSITORY, convert on checkout only where the tooling needs it, and mark the
# handful of formats where git must not touch the bytes at all.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Default: text files are stored with LF, whatever the contributor's OS.
*               text=auto eol=lf

# ── Explicit LF in the repository, CRLF in the Windows working tree ──────────
# These are the files Windows tooling genuinely requires to be CRLF. Listing
# them is better than setting `core.autocrlf=true` globally, which also rewrites
# files that must not be rewritten (shell scripts, in particular, break when
# given CRLF because /bin/sh chokes on the stray carriage return).
*.bat          text eol=crlf
*.cmd          text eol=crlf
*.ps1          text eol=crlf

# ── Binary: never touched, never diffed, never line-ending converted ────────
*.png          binary
*.jpg          binary
*.jpeg         binary
*.gif          binary
*.ico          binary
*.pdf          binary
*.zip          binary
*.gz           binary
*.ttf          binary
*.otf          binary
*.woff         binary
*.woff2        binary
*.so           binary
*.pyc          binary
*.pem          text eol=lf
*.key          text eol=lf

# ── Generated / vendored: tracked but not ours to reformat ─────────────────
# Lockfiles are generated by the package manager and must match byte-for-byte
# what it produced, or a diff here means a dependency actually changed.
pubspec.lock           text eol=lf
**/pubspec.lock        text eol=lf
package-lock.json      text eol=lf
**/package-lock.json   text eol=lf

# Migrations are applied in order to a live database and their exact content is
# recorded in the alembic version table. Reformatting one changes its checksum
# and makes `alembic` report an unexpected state.
backend/alembic/versions/*.py    text eol=lf

# Infrastructure is applied by Terraform, which is sensitive to whitespace and
# reads these files directly from a Linux runner.
infrastructure/**      text eol=lf

# ── Merge driver: never treat a Dart file as unmergeable ────────────────────
# Dart formatter differences between Flutter versions make git's built-in merge
# driver give up on two Dart files that changed different lines. Leaving it as
# `text` means git merges them; the formatter disagreement is then a one-line
# `dart format` rather than a manual three-way merge.
*.dart          text eol=lf diff=java
